A hidden, experimental `--member-invariants` option, requiring `--type-system-refresh`, begins member invariants (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/105). A datatype, class, trait, newtype or abstract type may declare `invariant e` members; they are parsed, printed and resolved as ghost predicates of the type, with the plan's reads restriction (an invariant depends on no mutable state); an invariant is never opaque. A codatatype may not declare an invariant, and a program, libraries included, that declares a member invariant may not declare a codatatype. For a datatype with invariants, `raw D` names the type of all its values, and `D` the subset of them that satisfy the invariants: constructor applications and updates have type `raw D`, and the members that an invariant applies to a raw value are inferred to be raw members, in which `this` has type `raw D`. The invariants of an inductive datatype with no type parameters and no parent traits, outside a refining module, are verified: converting a `raw D` to `D`, and applying a member that is not raw to a `raw D`, are checked against each invariant. Such a datatype may declare one `witness e` (or `ghost witness e`) member, which makes `D` auto-initializable (or nonempty), and gives compiled code its default value; without one, `D` is possibly empty. The resolver checks that an invariant does not refer to the set of values of its own type (the plan's extension condition), conservatively. A program that declares any other invariant is not verified: the verifier reports those invariants as not yet supported, and compiled code ignores invariants. `raw` for a class, trait, newtype or abstract type, and witness members of other types, are parsed but not yet supported. A `.doo` library built with the option requires it of its clients; one built without it is unchanged. Without the option, parsing, diagnostics and the generated Boogie are unchanged.
